From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 21:08:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make the hosted demo answer a visitor instead of an operator

QA pass over the public deployment as a first-time visitor with no
credential. The demo surface itself was already strong; what was not was
everything a visitor reaches when they click past it.

NO PUBLIC PATH ENDS AT AN ADMIN TOKEN

Network Bisect offered a target field, a repeat count, a strategy toggle
and an enabled cyan Isolate button. Pressing it produced a red error AND
opened the "Operator sign-in - ADMIN TOKEN" dialog. Live Diagnostics did
the same with a mode strip and a Run button. A visitor's reasonable
reading of that is "I need the developer's password to use this", which
is the one impression a portfolio demo cannot afford.

auth.promptUnlock() now refuses to open that dialog on a public demo, and
the operator control bars are not rendered there at all: those views lead
with the designed operator-surface state and a single action that works.
Every button and link reachable in the content area of all twelve views
was clicked; the credential dialog no longer appears from any of them.

The one place it should appear now does - Settings, which the rail
reaches at every width, because the narrow topbar drops the operator
button so the public call to action can keep its place.

Cases and the invitation dialog took the same treatment.

THE BRAND MARK POINTED AT A LOCKED PAGE

It linked to Overview, which on a hosted deployment renders "Live data is
locked". Clicking the logo from the demo therefore looked like hitting a
paywall. It points at the demo, and Overview now uses the shared
operator-surface state rather than credential language.

VANTAGE LANGUAGE

Four surfaces claimed a local reading a hosted deployment cannot take:

  Network Bisect  a "Measured locally" chip
  Topology        "Local topology inferred from routing, adapter and
                  neighbour evidence", with an empty state promising a map
                  that would appear after a run - and a CTA into a locked
                  page to get it
  Settings        "Local measurement - DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, ICMP, path",
                  tagged MEASURED, on a runtime with no raw socket
  Live/Environment  "ICMP and path" measurements and "off this machine"

Each now asks the runtime. words.measuredHere and words.thisMachine join
the existing vocabulary so this stays in one place, and the two static
paragraphs in index.html are gated by the attribute the server already
stamps onto <html>, so the first paint is correct without JavaScript.

A test pins all three phrases: they must be reachable only through a
runtime branch, never as a bare literal.

THE CAPSULE NEVER OPENED

Preserve is a third of the product model and its control says "Open the
capsule", but the route served content-disposition: attachment - so the
browser downloaded a file and left the tab blank. It is inline by
default, with ?download=1 for the file, and pinned behind a CSP that
matches what a capsule actually is: its own inline integrity check, no
network access, no framing.

REFUSED TARGETS NO LONGER SPEND THE DIAGNOSTIC BUDGET

Ten refused targets exhausted a visitor's ten live diagnostics. Since the
demo publishes its allowlist, discovering it by typing three hostnames
locked you out before you ran anything. A refusal decided on the string
alone - bad scheme, bad port, literal address, unlisted host - resolves
nothing and connects to nothing, so it is refunded and charged to a
separate, wider bucket instead. A refusal after resolution still costs a
slot, because that one did real work. Thirty SSRF probes in a row now
leave the eleventh legitimate diagnostic working.

MOBILE

At 390px the topbar's only two buttons were "Analyst" and "Operator
sign-in": the public call to action was the element being dropped for
space. It keeps its place now and the duplicated chrome goes instead. The
brand mark - a 20px square rotated 45deg, so 28px wide - was clipped by
4px against the viewport edge in the horizontal rail, and the demo flag
collided with the truncated page title.

In the stacked sample table, a cell like `FAIL <small>ENETUNREACH</small>`
is two grid items, and the second was auto-placing onto a new row under
the label - the table read "TARGET / ENETUNREACH" with the state stranded
beside it.

ALSO

- withTimeout unref'd its timer, so a budget whose only pending work was
  the timer could be skipped entirely and never fire. It also made the
  test for it fail. Both settle paths clear the timer, so the unref was
  buying nothing.
- HTTP PASS means the exchange completed, which is the right answer for a
  connectivity tool - a 403 from a bot filter is not a network fault. But
  green beside a 4xx reads as Faultline calling it healthy, so a
  non-success status now says what the PASS does and does not cover.
- Two links pointed at github.com/weegienamja/Faultline, which is not
  this repository.
- The demo status line and the live status line are aria-live regions;
  neither result nor error was announced before.

Verified against a hosted-runtime server: 521 tests pass, 2 skipped for
Ollama. Thirty SSRF and malformed-target cases refused, every admin
endpoint still 401, no console errors and no horizontal overflow at 390,
768, 1024, 1440 and 1920.
